-- germany.wuerttemberg
insert into public.wine_place_articles (wine_place_id, description, climate, soils, key_facts, editorial_status)
select id, 'Germany''s fourth-largest region at 10.694 hectares, and the one that turned red: 65 per cent of the vineyard is planted to red varieties, where Germany as a whole is 30,7 per cent red. The vines follow the Neckar and the sheltered valleys of its tributaries — the Rems, Enz, Kocher, Jagst and Tauber — and reach Lake Constance in the south. Its heart is the Bereich Württembergisches Unterland on the middle Neckar, with Remstal-Stuttgart just south of it. Steep sites make up an unusually high share, worked in small parcels, and more than fifty co-operatives market about 80 per cent of the wine.', 'Continental and sheltered rather than mild. The region depends on its river valleys: the Neckar and its tributaries cut warm, south-facing slopes into the surrounding uplands and moderate the nights, which is what lets red varieties ripen this far north and east. Away from the water the Swabian plateau is too cold and too frost-prone for vines.', 'Mostly Keuper and Muschelkalk, and which one matters. The middle Neckar and the Remstal sit on Keuper marls and sandstones — gypsum Keuper and Schilfsandstein — which hold warmth and suit Trollinger and Lemberger. The Kocher, Jagst and Tauber valleys to the north-east run on Muschelkalk, the shell limestone that gives the firmer Rieslings. Loess covers both in places.', array['10.694 ha (DWI, 2025), Germany''s fourth-largest region', '65 % of the vineyard is red — 6.721 ha — where Germany as a whole is 30,7 % red', 'Riesling is nevertheless the single most planted variety, at 1.995 ha', 'Lemberger (1.661 ha) has overtaken Trollinger (1.614 ha) as the most planted red', 'Schillerwein, fermented from red and white grapes together, is a Württemberg speciality', 'More than 50 co-operatives market roughly 80 % of the region''s wine']::text[], 'PUBLISHED'
  from public.wine_places where canonical_key = 'germany.wuerttemberg';
